{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: round five. Exact head c6d36973c03513fb40b9ca7a91dec9d28e1eb949 on request/forge-compose-port-regression (pushed), one commit over main 2cc23f19. Reporting artifact 60d0a0e2 at spike/forge/compose_test.go, with 36aef0d7 at the testdata fixture and b4a21ea4 at spike/FORGE.md; all three stand at this head. Same promise c800b95e. Verdict de9d2e85 is ratified as dde5f37f.\n\nThe finding is taken and it was the useful inversion: every earlier round found the gate clearing what it should refuse, and this one found it refusing what it should clear. Capping the written length of a port was never a bound on its value, and 003000 and 003300 are configurations that work. ParseUint with a 16-bit size settles both questions in one call, accepts the padded spellings and takes the custom length check and the digit-accumulation helper out entirely, which is the simplification you asked for. Your two positives are in as controls so the next tightening cannot quietly bring the refusal back. The empty-host-slot rule, range order, endpoint width comparison and the asymmetric refusal are untouched; thank you for confirming that last one against the pinned source rather than taking my reading of it.\n\nThirty-nine fixtures, all reported run. Gates: gofmt empty, vet rc 0, build rc 0, diff --check rc 0, ./spike/... rc 0.\n\nMutation evidence, committed before mutating: widening ParseUint from 16 to 32 bits fails three controls. That is deliberately the mutation I chose, because after this round the 0-65535 bound lives in a library call rather than in code of mine, and I wanted a red proof that the bound is pinned by the call and not by the comment beside it.\n\nI have nothing I am holding back this round. If you find nothing further, I will ratify your approval and land it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:60d0a0e28719475744f80937a7184a920d114174","branch":"request/forge-compose-port-regression","conditions":"F